---
const stages = [
  ['01', 'PunchOut started', 'The buyer launches your catalog from their procurement system. PunchOut Central identifies the buyer connection and opens the appropriate Shopify catalog.'],
  ['02', 'Cart returned', 'The buyer selects items and returns the cart to procurement. This is a requisition for review, not a Shopify order.'],
  ['03', 'Buyer approved', 'The buyer completes the approval process in their procurement system. Approval rules belong to the buyer.'],
  ['04', 'PO received', 'PunchOut Central receives the approved purchase order and connects it to the earlier session and cart.'],
  ['05', 'Store order created', 'The approved PO creates the corresponding order in the supplier’s Shopify store.'],
  ['06', 'Invoice generated', 'Invoice activity is tied to the buyer, PO and store order in the transaction record.']
];
---
<main>
  <section class="page-hero page-hero--mint" id="overview">
    <div class="wrap page-hero__grid">
      <div class="page-hero__copy reveal">
        <span class="ey">How PunchOut Central works</span>
        <h1>From procurement request to Shopify order and invoice.</h1>
        <p class="lead">A buyer shops your existing Shopify catalog from procurement. The cart returns for approval; the Shopify order follows only after the approved purchase order arrives.</p>
        <div class="page-hero__actions"><a class="btn pri" href="/book-demo/">Discuss your buyer workflow &rarr;</a><a class="btn secbtn" href="#details">Follow the six stages &darr;</a></div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-visual reveal">
        <div class="hero-visual__label"><span>Transaction journey</span><b>Six stages</b></div>
        <div class="visual-flow">
          {stages.map(([number, title]) => <div class="visual-step"><i>{number}</i><span><strong>{title}</strong><small>Connected transaction stage</small></span><em>{number}</em></div>)}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="sec" id="details">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head center reveal"><span class="ey">The full journey</span><h2>Six stages, with approval in the right place.</h2><p class="lead">The supplier storefront, buyer procurement system and PunchOut Central each have a distinct role.</p></div>
      <div class="simple-steps">{stages.map(([number, title, description]) => <article class="simple-step reveal"><span class="simple-step__num">{number}</span><h3>{title}</h3><p>{description}</p></article>)}</div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="sec soft" id="rule">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal"><span class="ey">Order boundary</span><h2>Cart return does not complete checkout.</h2><p class="lead">The buyer’s procurement system handles approval. PunchOut Central waits for the approved PO before creating the Shopify order.</p></div>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article class="feature-card reveal"><span class="feature-card__icon">01</span><h3>Buyer starts in procurement</h3><p>The procurement system initiates the session and receives the selected cart back.</p></article>
        <article class="feature-card reveal"><span class="feature-card__icon">02</span><h3>Supplier catalog stays in Shopify</h3><p>Buyers shop the products and collections available to their connection in your existing store.</p></article>
        <article class="feature-card reveal"><span class="feature-card__icon">03</span><h3>Approved PO starts fulfillment</h3><p>The PO is the handoff that creates the store order, with invoice activity tracked afterward.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="sec" id="proof">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal"><span class="ey">Transaction visibility</span><h2>Follow the connection across systems.</h2><p class="lead">View the buyer, session, cart, PO, store order and invoice context together, including issues that need attention.</p></div>
      <div class="related-grid">
        <a class="related-card reveal" href="/platform/transactions/"><span>Platform</span><h3>Transaction tracking</h3><p>See the status and history of a procurement transaction.</p><b>Explore &rarr;</b></a>
        <a class="related-card reveal" href="/integrations/shopify/"><span>Storefront</span><h3>Shopify integration</h3><p>Learn how the existing supplier store fits the workflow.</p><b>Explore &rarr;</b></a>
        <a class="related-card reveal" href="/integrations/procurement-systems/"><span>Buyer systems</span><h3>Procurement compatibility</h3><p>Review buyer-side systems and connection requirements.</p><b>Explore &rarr;</b></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="sec soft" id="faq">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head center reveal"><span class="ey">FAQ</span><h2>Workflow questions</h2></div>
      <div class="faq reveal">
        <div class="fi"><button class="fq" type="button"><span>Does returning the cart place an order?</span><span class="plus">+</span></button><div class="fa"><p>No. The cart goes back to procurement for approval. The Shopify order is created after the approved PO is received.</p></div></div>
        <div class="fi"><button class="fq" type="button"><span>Where does the buyer shop?</span><span class="plus">+</span></button><div class="fa"><p>In the supplier’s existing Shopify or Shopify Plus store, with catalog access configured for that buyer.</p></div></div>
        <div class="fi"><button class="fq" type="button"><span>Can we see where a transaction stopped?</span><span class="plus">+</span></button><div class="fa"><p>PunchOut Central provides transaction status, history, issue visibility and reporting across the workflow.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="page-cta"><div class="wrap reveal"><span class="ey">Plan the connection</span><h2>Walk through your buyer’s requirements.</h2><p class="lead">Share the procurement system and Shopify store you need to connect.</p><div class="page-hero__actions"><a class="btn pri" href="/book-demo/">Book a conversation &rarr;</a><a class="btn secbtn" href="/pricing/">See pricing</a></div></div></section>
</main>
